					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1909832&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606360628" alt="KholetteDrake" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1909832)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kholettedrake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1909832); return false;" >KholetteDrake</a></span> <span class="odate time_1434483320 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Jun 2015 19:35</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2318508">
									

<p>Well. Consider me officially squicked out. I suspect the rape is going to get a lot of discussion, though. For my part, I can see the relevance. From a human point of view, it was exactly that. LurkD handled it well, though, keeping everything clinical and avoiding a more graphic depiction that probably would have been less acceptable. I can also see it from 2401's perspective, though, and that's the part that horrifies me. It wasn't a woman raping a man, it was a queen mating with a drone. The final interview makes it pretty clear that it's no longer human and hasn't been for some time. Just because it was able to act that way to lure Lamb into a false sense of safety does not make it less of a monster. It behaved as a queen bee does, limited by its humanoid biology.</p>
<p>Edit: That first image still makes me cringe.</p>

